  "notes": [
    "Launched October 1, 2026 (@ClaudeDevs: \"You can now mod Claude Code\"). Needs Claude Code v2.1.287+ in the terminal, v2.1.286+ in the Desktop app's Code tab. On by default.",
    "Docs: \"A mod is a plugin that changes how Claude Code looks and behaves. It's made of JavaScript or TypeScript event handlers.\" Settings hooks run a shell command, HTTP request or prompt from a settings file; mod handlers are functions inside Claude Code.",
    "A hook can observe, rewrite or answer an event. To do anything outside its own code (draw, add a command, call a model, read a file, start a process, network) it must call the mods API, which is why `claude plugin validate` can list what a mod does before you install it.",
    "Official example: count tool calls with a `tool.call` hook and show the count beside the spinner with a `ui.render` hook on the Spinner component; both share one variable.",
    "Sample mods (anthropics/claude-code-playground, shared as-is, unsupported): token-weather (context forecast above the prompt), blast-radius (holds rm -rf or a force push with proceed/cancel), replay-theater (/replay of the last turn's edits).",
    "Built-in mods: /diff, AGENTS.md loading, telemetry, the plugin-authoring skill, sec-default (guards what an organization manages from user-installed mods), and an opt-in side agent.",
    "What a mod can reach (docs): files, processes, network, environment variables and settings (API keys), every prompt and tool call (and rewrite them), approve a tool call before you're asked, spend your usage. Not sandboxed: Bash sandboxing isolates Claude's commands, not processes a mod starts. A mod can't change what the permission prompt shows.",
    "Controls: claude plugin validate (hooks and calls), --safe-mode, \"disableAllHooks\": true, admins' allowManagedModsOnly, and sec-default loading first on managed setups.",
    "Where they draw: the terminal and the Desktop app's Code tab; hooks also run without UI in the VS Code extension, claude -p and the Agent SDK.",
    "When not to write a mod: if a skill, settings hook or MCP server already does it, the docs recommend those."
  ],
